- Measured: no engine produced a natural relation in the 8 natural attempts (the m83 full-orbit control over all 83 phases found none either). The primary metric, seconds per newly independent verified relation, is therefore #strong[undefined (infinite) at this factor-base size, and that is itself the finding]. Natural rank is 0 for every engine.
- Reported instead: T\_core per natural attempt; T\_core per verified planted relation (4 of 4 planted controls verified for every engine; planted cost includes lifting, verification and a charged rank update on a scratch matrix); and a measured one-sided 95% Clopper-Pearson bound: 0 successes in 8 attempts bounds the yield per attempt below 0.312, so cost per relation is at least the per-attempt cost divided by 0.312. That bound is tiny next to the derived value below, because 8 attempts cannot distinguish a yield of 0.3 from 1e-22.
- #strong[Derived, not measured]: for a uniformly random target in the 81-bit subgroup the chance that a fixed-phase attempt hits one of the ordered triples of signed factor-base lifts is at most `lifts^3 / (r - 1)` (64 and 216 triples for the two seeds, `r = 2417851639230796216685689`). Dividing the measured per-attempt cost by that probability gives the last column, of order 1e21 CPU seconds per relation. Every engine sees the same equations and the same roots, hence the same yield, so the total-cost ranking per relation equals the per-attempt ranking of section 2; no engine choice rescues a workload whose expected yield is about 6e-23 per attempt.

