- #strong[The dominant component flips with the workload.] At n \= 13 the solver stage is 99.9% (CryptoMiniSat) and 100.0% (Z3) of the charged cost, against 0.5%-66% for the six-variable m \= 83 systems of W1a. Everything outside the solver together costs 139.7 ms (CryptoMiniSat) and 112.8 ms (Z3) of CPU per four targets, so here engine choice is essentially the whole cost. This compares workload scale, not engine merit.
- #strong[Here the metric is finite.] These targets are natural and decompose, so CryptoMiniSat produced 4 independent verified natural relations (rank 4 over Z/2003, rows re-reduced independently by `verify.py`) at 32.2 CPU s per relation. Z3 produced 2 in 325.4 CPU s: 162.7 CPU s per relation, i.e. 5.1x CryptoMiniSat\'s. That figure is a #strong[lower bound]: the two timed-out targets are charged the 118-120 s they consumed, and their true cost is higher (right-censored).
- On the two targets both engines solved, Z3 took 1.2x and 1.4x CryptoMiniSat\'s time; on the other two CryptoMiniSat took 12 s and 50 s and Z3 reached its deadline. Four targets, one trial each, and an unconfigurable CryptoMiniSat seed: the per-target spread is as large as the engine gap on the solved pair, so only the coarse statement is supported (CryptoMiniSat with native XOR solved all four, Z3 with Xor terms two).
- The comparison pits native XOR against XOR flattened to terms; it is not a statement about SAT solvers in general. The Z3 runs saw 1-minute load averages of about 2-3 against 1.3-2.0 during CryptoMiniSat\'s; CPU seconds are reported, but cache and frequency effects of a shared host are not controlled.
- This does not repeat Lead 1\'s paired phase-on/phase-off comparison on the same harness; it varies the engine, not the formulation.

